{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q026_protein_kinase_like_function_transfer","answer":"Verified SCOPe facts (release 2.08): protein kinase-like (PK-like) superfamily d.144.1 (sunid 56112) lies in fold d.144 (class d, alpha and beta proteins a+b). Its child families include d.144.1.4, Phosphoinositide 3-kinase (PI3K) catalytic domain (sunid 56171); d.144.1.6, APH phosphotransferases (sunid 64411), whose listed members include Aminoglycoside 3'-phosphotransferase IIa (sunid 103302) and Type IIIa 3',5'-aminoglycoside phosphotransferase (sunid 64412); and d.144.1.7, Protein kinases, catalytic subunit (sunid 88854). The parent lineages of all three families run through d.144.1, and their homology responses report homology implied by level with no warnings or heterogeneity flags. The superfamily homology response also has no warnings. The p85 regulatory subunit of PI3K is classified outside d.144 (search labels: d.93.1.1 for p85-alpha, h.4.21.1 for the iSH2 domain, b.34.2.1 for the SH3 domain); those labels come from search results and I did not fetch their full lineages.

Interpretation: SCOPe places the three catalytic domains in one superfamily, so it supports probable common ancestry of a PK-like catalytic core at superfamily level. The three are distinct families, and SCOPe makes no family-level claim linking, for example, APH to protein kinases. The supportable conclusion is a shared ancestral catalytic architecture, not shared substrates, reaction specificity, or regulation. A nucleotide-dependent phosphoryl-transfer mechanism is a general biochemical expectation from my background knowledge; SCOPe does not establish it, and it needs structural or biochemical sources before it is cited. These three should not be described as nonhomologous, because SCOPe makes a positive superfamily-level homology assertion.

Limits and unsafe transfers: Substrate specificity does not follow from family or superfamily placement. The aminoglycoside substrate names apply to the APH members named in the labels and do not carry over to protein kinases or PI3K. Within the protein kinases, substrate class (for example Ser/Thr versus Tyr) must be checked against that target's own residues. Before transferring specificity, one needs (1) complex structures with the substrate or a validated docking model; (2) a residue-level alignment mapping the substrate-recognition and catalytic residues of the target to the reference; (3) kinetic or substrate-panel data with the actual substrate; and (4) mutational tests of the proposed specificity determinants. Regulation does not transfer from the catalytic superfamily. Activation-loop phosphorylation, autoinhibitory or regulatory domains, binding partners such as the p85 subunit for PI3K, and pseudokinase status all require their own evidence: regulatory-domain structures or classifications, phosphorylation and binding-partner data, and lipid-substrate data for PI3K. A superfamily placement says nothing about the full domain boundaries or about other domains in a chain. If a structure is used as a template, quality values must be retrieved for every candidate before any ranking; I did not rank candidates. I also did not retrieve sequence or structure hits for any unknown target, so no claim is made about a specific new sequence.","evidence_used":["mcp__scope__search_scop","mcp__scope__get_scop_node","mcp__scope__list_scop_children","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=aminoglycoside+phosphotransferase&mode=auto&limit=10","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=phosphatidylinositol+3-kinase&mode=auto&limit=10","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/d.144.1","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/d.144.1/children?limit=30","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/56112/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/88854","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/88854/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/88854/homology"